format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-and-the-body

objective u4-cell-repair

statement:
    Know that cells are very good at repairing radiation damage, and that damage which is not repaired correctly can kill a cell or eventually make it cancerous.

misconception: Radiation damage can never be repaired.

item u4-l2-choice-not-repaired
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-health-effects

prompt:
    What can happen if the damage is not repaired correctly?

options:
    {"id":"die","label":"The cell may die or eventually become cancerous"}
    {"id":"fine","label":"Nothing: damage is always harmless","note":"If damage is not repaired correctly, a cell may die or eventually become cancerous."}
    {"id":"radio","label":"The person becomes radioactive","note":"Exposure does not make a person radioactive. The risk is to the cell itself."}

answer: die

explanation:
    If damage is not repaired correctly, a cell may die or eventually become cancerous. That is why repair matters.

item u4-l2-choice-puzzle
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-health-effects

prompt:
    Radiation can damage DNA, yet the small doses of everyday life cause no immediate illness. Which is part of the answer?

options:
    {"id":"repair","label":"Cells are very good at repairing damage, and everyday doses are small"}
    {"id":"miss","label":"Everyday radiation never reaches any cell","note":"Everyone is exposed to ionizing radiation every day, so some does reach cells."}
    {"id":"harm","label":"The damage is harmless, so repair is not needed","note":"Repair matters, because damage that is not repaired correctly can lead to a dead or cancerous cell."}

answer: repair

explanation:
    The cells in our bodies are extremely efficient at repairing damage, and low levels of radiation do not cause immediate health effects.

item u4-l2-match-ideas
type: match
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-health-effects

prompt:
    Match each idea to what it says about a cell.

pairs:
    {"id":"dam","left":"Radiation hits the DNA","right":"The cell is damaged"}
    {"id":"rep","left":"Cells are extremely efficient at repair","right":"Most damage is put right"}
    {"id":"not","left":"Damage is not repaired correctly","right":"The cell may die or become cancerous"}

explanation:
    Damage comes first, repair usually follows, and only damage that is not repaired correctly causes lasting harm.

item u4-l2-tom-repair-efficient
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-health-effects

statement:
    The cells in our bodies are extremely efficient at repairing radiation damage.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. Cells repair this kind of damage all the time, which is why a small everyday dose does not make anyone ill.

item u4-x-tom-repair-true
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-health-effects

statement:
    The cells in our bodies are very good at repairing damage from radiation.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. The cells in our bodies are extremely efficient at repairing this damage.
